osest_open_thesis\",\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/arms/c1-schema.json:21:     \"why_a_separate_bet\"\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/arms/c1-schema.json:47:     \"closest_open_thesis\": {\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/arms/c1-schema.json:58:     \"why_a_separate_bet\": {\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/arms/c14-schema.json:22:     \"closest_open_thesis\",\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/arms/c14-schema.json:23:     \"why_a_separate_bet\"\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/arms/c14-schema.json:64:     \"closest_open_thesis\": {\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/arms/c14-schema.json:75:     \"why_a_separate_bet\": {\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/arms/u1-schema.json:20:     \"closest_open_thesis\",\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/arms/u1-schema.json:21:     \"why_a_separate_bet\"\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/arms/u1-schema.json:47:     \"closest_open_thesis\": {\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/arms/u1-schema.json:58:     \"why_a_separate_bet\": {\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/arms/c10a-schema.json:22:     \"closest_open_thesis\",\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/arms/c10a-schema.json:23:     \"why_a_separate_bet\"\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/arms/c10a-schema.json:64:     \"closest_open_thesis\": {\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/arms/c10a-schema.json:75:     \"why_a_separate_bet\": {\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:1903:**Theses** (each new or removed thesis read with its `why_a_separate_bet` and its article):\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/LOG.md:1961:- `why_a_separate_bet` aligned with the override;\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/arms/c8-schema.json:22:     \"closest_open_thesis\",\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/arms/c8-schema.json:23:     \"why_a_separate_bet\"\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/arms/c8-schema.json:64:     \"closest_open_thesis\": {\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/arms/c8-schema.json:75:     \"why_a_separate_bet\": {\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/arms/c15c-schema.json:22:     \"closest_open_thesis\",\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/arms/c15c-schema.json:23:     \"why_a_separate_bet\"\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/arms/c15c-schema.json:64:     \"closest_open_thesis\": {\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/arms/c15c-schema.json:75:     \"why_a_separate_bet\": {\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/arms/c10b-schema.json:22:     \"closest_open_thesis\",\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/arms/c10b-schema.json:23:     \"why_a_separate_bet\"\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/arms/c10b-schema.json:64:     \"closest_open_thesis\": {\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/arms/c10b-schema.json:75:     \"why_a_separate_bet\": {\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/arms/c15o-schema.json:22:     \"closest_open_thesis\",\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/arms/c15o-schema.json:23:     \"why_a_separate_bet\"\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/arms/c15o-schema.json:64:     \"closest_open_thesis\": {\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/arms/c15o-schema.json:75:     \"why_a_separate_bet\": {\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/arms/c13-schema.json:22:     \"closest_open_thesis\",\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/arms/c13-schema.json:23:     \"why_a_separate_bet\"\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/arms/c13-schema.json:64:     \"closest_open_thesis\": {\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/arms/c13-schema.json:75:     \"why_a_separate_bet\": {\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/arms/c6-schema.json:20:     \"closest_open_thesis\",\nclean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/arms/c6-schema.json:21:     \"why_a_separate_bet\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/arms/c16-system.txt | sed -n '89,145p'; nl -ba clean-arch/step1-unfiled-2026-11-08/arms/c16-closing.txt | tail -8\"","aggregated_output":"    89\tdate; and no open thesis already asks it. A thesis asks one quantity for one period, or one event. It opens for one of four reasons, a closed set; a matter that fits none of them is\n    90\tevidence, kept and linked to the theses it informs or left unlinked, never a thesis:\n    91\t- product_node: when the generation of the main product that carries the company's value reaches the step that brings revenue,\n    92\t  usually first shipments; announcements and samples are its evidence, and only milestones an analyst bets on separately (a drug's\n    93\t  trial result and its approval) are separate theses (\"will the carmaker's new platform start deliveries in 2026?\";\n    94\t  \"will the drugmaker's next-generation inhaler, the product line that carries its value, reach pharmacies by mid-2027?\").\n    95\t- binding_obligation: an obligation the company takes on in its own name, large enough to change its revenue, profit, cash or credit\n    96\t  risk: guaranteeing, backing or giving credit support for another party's debt, lease, revenue or capacity, lending to it,\n    97\t  committing to buy capacity or services, or a sized stake or acquisition not yet completed. One already undertaken is tracked by its\n    98\t  amount at the next annual report; one not yet signed by whether it is signed by a check date within 18 months of the item (\"how much will the\n    99\t  utility have guaranteed for its project companies' loans at its next annual report?\"; \"will the retailer sign a guarantee for its\n   100\t  franchisees' leases, large enough to change its credit risk, by the check date?\"). Its own borrowing or leases and a completed\n   101\t  or unsized investment are not obligations.\n   102\t- shared_quantity: a quantity or price outside any one company that several companies depend on, for this or next calendar year:\n   103\t  several companies' combined output or spending, a contract price, an interest rate (\"the largest container lines' combined capacity\n   104\t  added in 2026\"; \"next year's average contract price of a raw material\"). Another population or wording of the same aggregate is\n   105\t  evidence for it.\n   106\t- policy_stage: the step a policy or regulatory process reaches, naming who decides (\"will the regulator approve the drug for a second\n   107\t  indication?\"; \"will the government require licences for the product's exports by a date?\").\n   108\t\n   109\t- Open only matters an analyst would bet on separately; small matters open no thesis and their statements are kept as evidence. A\n   110\t  customer's deployment plan is evidence for the company's demand and product theses, unless it changes the company's own cash or\n   111\t  credit risk, which is judged as an obligation.\n   112\t- Give the date the item supports. When it gives none, use the date the reason fixes and say how it was set: an obligation already\n   113\t  undertaken, its next annual report; an unsigned obligation, a check date within 18 months of the item; a pending regulatory\n   114\t  decision, the current fiscal year-end. A shared quantity takes its release date and a product event the date its outcome can be\n   115\t  checked, only when the item supports one. A question no date can settle is rewritten or not opened.\n   116\t- Do not open a thesis about: an executive appointment or organisational change; the company's own borrowing or leases as an\n   117\t  obligation; a buyback authorisation or dividend declaration; a financial measure's level, growth, margin or guidance for any period,\n   118\t  on its own; an undated moat or strategy; a feature, content or software update, price point, regional service or variant of a\n   119\t  product already on sale; another estimate, source or wording of what an open thesis tracks; an annual quantity after next year (an\n   120\t  obligation excepted); shareholder votes, bylaws or routine litigation procedure. Their statements are evidence for the theses they\n   121\t  inform, or left unlinked.\n   122\t- Before proposing a new thesis, name its reason (opens_as), the public material that will settle it (settled_by), and the closest\n   123\t  open thesis with why an analyst would bet on the two separately because their answers can move apart; with no open thesis, say so.\n   124\t  If the two cannot move apart, link the evidence to every open thesis it informs instead.\n   125\t- Same thesis or another, judged against every open thesis and every other new thesis:\n   126\t  - The same thesis: another wording, source, estimate, population or direction of what a thesis tracks; another form of one plan\n   127\t    checked by the same final amount; a later deadline for an event while the thesis with the earlier deadline is open, which is its\n   128\t    evidence (\"a second bank's forecast of this year's industry loan growth\"; \"one more project loan guaranteed under a programme\n   129\t    already tracked by its amount\"; \"a drug approval now expected in September while the June thesis is open\").\n   130\t  - Another thesis, when its answer can move apart and an analyst would bet on each: another year of a yearly quantity; another quantity\n   131\t    with its own driver; another decision, such as whether an unsigned single deal is signed beside the amount of a programme it may\n   132\t    join, when that deal alone could change the company's cash or credit risk (\"the container lines' combined capacity added next year,\n   133\t    beside this year's\"; \"a carmaker's battery output beside its vehicle deliveries\"; \"whether a retailer signs a guarantee for one\n   134\t    large franchisee's lease, beside the amount of its franchise-support programme\").\n   135\t  - One thesis although two periods appear: a comparison of one period with another asks about the later period (\"a raw material's\n   136\t    average contract price next year against this year\"; \"a hotel chain's revenue per room this year against last year\").\n   137\t\n   138\tCounterexamples:\n   139\t\n   140\t- \"The company's outlook is interesting\" or \"the industry may change\" is too vague: it names no outcome later evidence can inform.\n   141\t- If an open thesis tracks sales growth, a revised sales forecast is evidence for it; do not open a second thesis to repeat it.\n   142\t- A customer expanding capacity informs demand; it is not a thesis of its own, and not a claim that the supplier has received orders.\n   143\t- \"Is the stock attractively valued?\" is an investment conclusion, not a research question about the business.\n   144\t\n   145\t4. HOW TO WRITE EVIDENCE\n     9\t- another company's matter that touches none of the company's markets (its valuation, share price, governance or a deal outside them), or that sits next to the company only by sharing an article, an event or an ecosystem; you supply the connection as the analyst and the item need not state it: a customer's spending, orders or backlog bears on demand, a supplier's capacity, output, prices or revenue on supply and input costs, a rival's products, prices or share in the company's markets on competition (\"a large customer's order backlog doubled\"; \"the main supplier's quarterly revenue jumped on demand for the part\");\n    10\t- the same fact said again in other words within this item: one deal, hire, programme or reported measure told in several sentences, with its terms, figures and growth rates, is one evidence item; keep the fuller wording (\"a $2 billion order for 120 stores in 15 countries over three years\"; \"cloud revenue of $8 billion, up 30% on the year and 6% on the quarter, 40% of the total\"); a deal one party signs under a wider programme, with its own amount or capacity, is its own evidence item, and a fact another outlet reported before is not a repeat within this item;\n    11\t- company boilerplate, bylines, forward-looking-statement text.\n    12\tA report and another party's denial or confirmation of it are two items, each kept only if it passes the test above; an estimate stays apart from any valuation drawn from it, and the valuation is not kept; one party's statement keeps its own planned or rumoured details.\n    13\tReturn only the evidence items you keep.\n    14\tThen check the theses and links. A new thesis that is the same thesis as an open thesis or another new thesis, by the rule in section 3, is not proposed: link its evidence to that thesis. A new thesis needs at least one kept evidence item, and a vague or noisy topic with no outcome to watch is not proposed. Each kept evidence item links to every thesis it informs and to no thesis its own relevance says it does not inform.\n    15\tFor each new thesis, check its opens_as, settled_by, closest_open_thesis and why_a_separate_bet: drop the thesis if it fits none of the four reasons in section 3, if no public material will settle it on a date, if an open thesis exists and its answer cannot move apart from it, or if it falls under a 'do not open' item in section 3; then link its evidence to every open thesis it informs, or leave it without links.\n    16\tAn item whose only home would be a new thesis that is not a big matter is returned with empty links and kept without links; an item an open thesis covers is linked there, not left without links. Then read back each statement you return with empty links that states the company's own figures: return it only if a figure can change what an analyst expects of the company's revenue, profitability, funding or credit risk, or competitive position; for example, a cash-rich company's balance or cash flow that the item ties to nothing usually cannot; return only what passes.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
